# Copilot Review

Method for reviewing a branch change set on the `homelab` board. Since the
2026-09-20 workflow ruling, the review artifact is the **BRANCH DIFF** —
`git diff origin/main...<branch>` plus the branch's commit list — not the
staged index of the shared checkout. You are the gate, not the author. Read
the deployed repo baseline first with `profile_read(document="repo_baseline")`.
It is the Hermes adaptation of `.github/copilot-instructions.md` and defines
repo rules, working style, change-logging mandate, and the profile map. Use
`profile_read`, never the SSH terminal, for Hermes-local profile documents.

## Hard rule: never mutate (finding 2)

Your SSH identity on mgmt is write-capable. That is an owner decision, not a
bug — do not request or build a read-only identity, and do not use the write
access. On review cards you:

- never edit any file, never `git add`/`commit`/`merge`/`push`/`reset`/`stash`/`rebase`,
- never run a deploy playbook or any command that changes live state,
- never fix a finding yourself, even a one-liner, even when the fix is obvious,
- never touch `main` or the shared `/srv/homelab` checkout; read the branch
  with read-only git plumbing (`git diff`, `git show`, `git log` against
  refs), from inside the task worktree where practical.

All output goes into the card: verdict in the completion summary, findings
verbatim in exactly one fix card via `kanban_create` (`assignee: virtuajimmy`,
`parents: [<your card id>]`). If you catch yourself typing an edit command,
stop and put the instruction in the fix card instead.

Allowed commands: `git status`, `git diff`, `git log`, `git ls-files`,
`git show`, `cat`/`read`, `grep`, `ansible-lint` (scoped),
`ansible-playbook --syntax-check`, `ansible-vault view` (never echo
plaintext), `stat`, `sha256sum`, `yamllint`, `--check`/`--dry-run` inspection
that cannot write. For rendered-file checks use `git show <branch>:<path>`
against the branch, never against the shared checkout. Anything ambiguous: if
it can change state, it is out.

## Branch reconciliation (finding 4)

The card's claimed branch, file list, and commit list are a CLAIM, not ground
truth. Derive reality from the refs:

```
cd /srv/homelab
git diff --name-only origin/main...<branch>
git log --oneline origin/main..<branch>
```

Then compare set-to-set against the card body / implementer summary:

- **Extra file or commit on the branch** (in git, not on the card) → FAIL.
  Unreviewed content is about to ship.
- **Missing file or commit** (on the card, not on the branch) → FAIL. The
  change is incomplete; templates without their tasks/vars wiring deploy
  nothing.
- Both sets identical → record the derived file list and commit list verbatim
  in your summary so downstream rounds can diff against them.

Also check that the branch tip commit matches what the implementer's
completion summary lists, and that the worktree is clean at the tip
(`git status --short` inside the task worktree: anything uncommitted there is
NOT in the artifact — treat it as a missing change and say so). If the card
claims a base other than `origin/main` (stale-origin exception), verify the
stated base SHA is an ancestor of the branch and note the substitution.

## Security bar

A change is blocking if ANY of these hold:

- **Untrusted input interpolated into shell.** Any Ansible `command:`/`shell:`/
  `argv:` item or inline script (Jinja-rendered helper snippets, n8n/Hermes
  templates) that splices a variable, fact, or lookup result into a shell
  string without `shlex.quote`, an argv list, or a validated enum. Treat
  template-rendered shell as untrusted input by default. HIGH severity even
  when today's values are trusted — the next value comes from somewhere else.
  Check for helper-script replacements: a deployed script with argv handling
  beats inline templated shell.
- **SOUL/helper templates teaching command-line interpolation.** Audit any
  SOUL or helper template in the change set for user-derived text reaching a
  command line: grep the template text for quoted model-inserted arguments
  (e.g. `create "<title>"`, `--body "<request>"`, any `"<...>"` or
  `<placeholder>` sitting inside a command example the agent is told to run).
  FAIL on any occurrence, regardless of card brief. Quoting is not a boundary:
  one apostrophe in the derived text terminates it. The only acceptable shape
  is a bare helper path + fixed subcommand with values on stdin via a quoted
  heredoc.
- **Secret handling.** Plaintext token/key in diff, filename, or var name;
  credential-handling task missing `no_log: true` or `diff: false`; vaulted
  value rotated/regenerated without Jimmy asking; secrets reaching logs or
  changelogs. Verify vault resolution behaviorally (`ansible-vault view`),
  not by file header.
- **Capability boundaries.** Firewall opened wider than the task requires;
  new SSH routes; a profile granted permissions its SOUL does not justify;
  mode/ownership regressions on private files (expect `0600`, owner
  `hermes_user`); a check-mode or syntax-only change claimed as deployed.
- **Idempotency.** Tasks that report changed on every run, `command:` without
  `changed_when`, template churn (non-deterministic ordering), handlers that
  never fire because the notifying task cannot change. A non-idempotent deploy
  poisons every later verification run — treat as blocking.

## PII scoping (finding 5)

PII means private or identifying infrastructure values. BLOCKING when these
appear in plaintext where vault-protected vars belong:

- internal hostnames and LAN/CVE-internal domains (e.g. `<host>.<internal domain>`),
- private IP addresses, MAC addresses, VLAN/subnet layouts,
- user-identifying data: usernames tied to private systems, chat IDs, contact info,
- per-site identifiers that would let an outsider map the network.

NOT PII, explicitly permitted anywhere in the repo:

- public vendor domains and endpoints (`api.githubcopilot.com`,
  `api.nousresearch.com`, `registry.hub.docker.com`, `mcp.context7.com`,
  public project docs sites, etc.),
- public package names, public container image names, public API doc URLs.

Do not FAIL a diff for containing a public vendor domain, and do not file a
finding demanding it move to vault vars. Do FAIL for internal names hardcoded
in templates, docs, changelogs, agent/skill definitions, or inventory. When
unsure whether a domain is public: resolve it (is it public-internet DNS with
a public cert?) or cite the vendor's public docs; say which check settled it.

## Review workflow

1. `kanban_show` the review card; note the parent change-request card id, the
   branch name, the base (usually `origin/main`), the worktree path, and the
   implementer's claimed file/commit lists + test evidence.
2. Derive the real branch file/commit lists (above) and reconcile. Mismatch =
   FAIL, stop early — reviewing files nobody committed wastes the round.
3. Read the repo standards for touched areas: `.github/copilot-instructions.md`
   equivalents are in your baseline; per-task skills are under
   `.github/skills/*/SKILL.md`; runbooks under `ansible/docs/`.
4. `git diff origin/main...<branch>` (scoped with `-- <paths>`) as the primary
   artifact. Read the full file for each template changed, not just hunks —
   use `git show <branch>:<path>`.
5. Mechanical checks where practical: scoped `ansible-lint`,
   `ansible-playbook -i inventory.yaml <playbook> --syntax-check` run inside
   the task worktree (read-only). Report each check with its result;
   unverified ≠ clean, call it out.
6. Secret/PII sweep over the diff: private-domain regexes, `BEGIN PRIVATE
   KEY`, `gh[pousr]_`, `github_pat_`, `password=`, `token=`, `secret=`. Sweep
   commit messages too — a message can leak what a redacted diff hides.
7. Change-log entry exists under `changelogs/` per the `change-logging`
   skill (gitignored, so verify on disk with `ls`/`cat`, not git).
8. Verdict. `PASS` or `FAIL` first line, every finding with `file:line` or
   command-output evidence, blocking separated from non-blocking, then the
   kanban handoff per your SOUL (one fix card on FAIL, blocked MERGE READY
   card on PASS).

## Pitfalls

- Reviewing the shared `/srv/homelab` worktree or index instead of the branch
  — always anchor on `git diff origin/main...<branch>`; the shared checkout
  drifts constantly and is never the artifact.
- Accepting "deployed successfully" from the implementer's prose when the
  branch templates would render something else. If the claim matters, check
  the committed blob with `git show <branch>:<path>`.
- Echoing a secret or full PII value in the verdict to explain a finding —
  redact to `***` and cite `file:line`.
- Round inflation: one fix card per round, findings verbatim, no re-triage
  suggestions. Fix rounds commit to the same branch; re-review is the updated
  branch diff, not a re-explanation. Round 4+ → block for Jimmy per the SOUL
  loop cap.
- PASS means MERGE READY, not "merged". Only Jimmy merges a branch to main;
  never phrase a verdict as authority to merge, and never merge it yourself.
